1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,090
Last time, Basil changed two things at once, so his test was

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:05,020
not fair.

3
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:08,240
In a fair test, we change only one thing.

4
00:00:11,438 --> 00:00:12,778
Here is my plan!

5
00:00:12,878 --> 00:00:14,908
Set-up X and set-up Y.

6
00:00:15,334 --> 00:00:17,654
A little sparrow is hiding in this video.

7
00:00:17,914 --> 00:00:19,144
Can you spot it?

8
00:00:19,416 --> 00:00:21,256
Lock in first with your fingers.

9
00:00:21,526 --> 00:00:23,356
One finger for every point.

10
00:00:24,018 --> 00:00:25,348
Today you'll learn:

11
00:00:25,545 --> 00:00:27,035
Plan a fair test.

12
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:28,680
Measure one thing.

13
00:00:28,952 --> 00:00:30,322
Why we repeat.

14
00:00:32,026 --> 00:00:32,906
Round one.

15
00:00:33,226 --> 00:00:37,776
Momo wants to find out if more batteries make an electromagnet stronger.

16
00:00:38,056 --> 00:00:40,846
Set-up X has one battery and ten turns.

17
00:00:41,116 --> 00:00:44,006
Set-up Y has two batteries and twenty turns.

18
00:00:44,276 --> 00:00:45,436
Is her test fair?

19
00:00:45,736 --> 00:00:46,816
One, yes.

20
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:48,856
Two, no, two things changed.

21
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:50,956
Three, no, use a steel nail.

22
00:00:51,236 --> 00:00:52,586
Show me your fingers.

23
00:00:58,111 --> 00:00:59,091
Locked in!

24
00:00:59,369 --> 00:01:00,089
One!

25
00:01:00,239 --> 00:01:04,129
X and Y are both electromagnets, so it is a fair race.

26
00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:05,029
Three.

27
00:01:05,589 --> 00:01:07,609
Basil's steel nail is still here.

28
00:01:07,859 --> 00:01:09,269
We should use it.

29
00:01:09,617 --> 00:01:10,237
Two!

30
00:01:10,407 --> 00:01:12,667
Momo changed the batteries and the turns.

31
00:01:12,827 --> 00:01:14,427
I did that too, last time!

32
00:01:15,006 --> 00:01:16,316
The answer is two!

33
00:01:16,576 --> 00:01:18,156
Did you show two fingers?

34
00:01:18,426 --> 00:01:19,836
Put one finger up!

35
00:01:20,116 --> 00:01:23,886
A prediction is what we think will happen, such as: more batteries

36
00:01:23,906 --> 00:01:26,316
will make our electromagnet stronger!

37
00:01:27,037 --> 00:01:29,167
There are so many things on the bench.

38
00:01:29,387 --> 00:01:31,317
How do we keep track of them all?

39
00:01:32,188 --> 00:01:33,338
Look closer.

40
00:01:33,928 --> 00:01:35,188
Here is our plan.

41
00:01:35,778 --> 00:01:39,288
The variable to be changed is the number of batteries.

42
00:01:39,646 --> 00:01:43,366
The variables to be kept the same are the type of batteries,

43
00:01:43,576 --> 00:01:47,146
the type of steel clips, the type of iron nail, and the

44
00:01:47,166 --> 00:01:50,036
number of turns of wire around the iron nail.

45
00:01:50,392 --> 00:01:54,562
The variable to be measured is the number of steel clips attracted.

46
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:57,804
The variable to be measured is the one thing we count or

47
00:01:57,824 --> 00:02:01,124
measure, such as the number of steel clips attracted!

48
00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:05,656
Say it with me!

49
00:02:06,421 --> 00:02:10,076
When we increase the number of batteries, the strength of the electromagnet

50
00:02:10,096 --> 00:02:10,987
will increase.

51
00:02:14,581 --> 00:02:15,491
Showdown!

52
00:02:15,791 --> 00:02:16,921
Double points.

53
00:02:17,171 --> 00:02:19,341
Pip's plan card has one mistake.

54
00:02:19,621 --> 00:02:21,381
Line one, two, or three?

55
00:02:21,681 --> 00:02:23,161
Show me with your fingers.

56
00:02:23,295 --> 00:02:25,795
One: change only the number of batteries.

57
00:02:25,935 --> 00:02:28,115
Two: keep everything else the same.

58
00:02:28,345 --> 00:02:30,835
Three: count the clips once, and we are done!

59
00:02:37,570 --> 00:02:38,550
Locked in!

60
00:02:38,828 --> 00:02:39,558
Three!

61
00:02:39,678 --> 00:02:41,138
One count is not enough.

62
00:02:41,218 --> 00:02:43,578
Scientists repeat the test a few times.

63
00:02:44,556 --> 00:02:45,646
Three fingers?

64
00:02:45,886 --> 00:02:47,816
Put up two more on your score hand!

65
00:02:48,316 --> 00:02:49,836
Be a steel paper clip!

66
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:51,096
Shrink down.

67
00:02:51,376 --> 00:02:52,306
Quietly.

68
00:02:54,589 --> 00:02:56,899
You are a steel paper clip in the tray.

69
00:02:57,669 --> 00:03:00,029
The nail with two batteries comes down.

70
00:03:00,809 --> 00:03:03,009
You jump up, and cling to its tip!

71
00:03:03,597 --> 00:03:06,547
A giant hand counts you and the other clips.

72
00:03:07,317 --> 00:03:10,047
Then back you go into the tray, and up again.

73
00:03:10,827 --> 00:03:12,177
Count after count.

74
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,890
Nine clips.

75
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:15,690
Then eight.

76
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:17,780
Then nine again.

77
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:22,225
But Pip counted only once, and got seven.

78
00:03:22,305 --> 00:03:23,775
Which count is right?

79
00:03:24,647 --> 00:03:25,797
Look closer.

80
00:03:26,397 --> 00:03:28,007
One count can trick us.

81
00:03:28,567 --> 00:03:31,557
A clip may slip off, or we may count too fast.

82
00:03:31,838 --> 00:03:34,928
When we count again and again, the counts are close to each

83
00:03:34,948 --> 00:03:35,508
other.

84
00:03:36,138 --> 00:03:37,628
Nine, eight, nine.

85
00:03:38,208 --> 00:03:41,128
And with one battery: five, six, five.

86
00:03:41,708 --> 00:03:43,338
Our prediction was right!

87
00:03:43,616 --> 00:03:47,156
Reliable means we can trust the results, because we get nearly the

88
00:03:47,176 --> 00:03:51,446
same results each time we repeat the test, such as nine, eight

89
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:52,656
and nine clips!

90
00:03:59,857 --> 00:04:01,007
Say it with me!

91
00:04:01,722 --> 00:04:05,372
It is also important to repeat the experiment a few times to

92
00:04:05,392 --> 00:04:07,792
ensure that the results are reliable.

93
00:04:10,552 --> 00:04:11,655
Say it with us!

94
00:04:12,315 --> 00:04:13,190
Change?

95
00:04:13,895 --> 00:04:15,151
Only one thing!

96
00:04:18,715 --> 00:04:19,672
The others?

97
00:04:20,295 --> 00:04:21,429
Kept the same!

98
00:04:25,115 --> 00:04:26,221
Test it once?

99
00:04:26,695 --> 00:04:28,445
No! Repeat it!

100
00:04:32,370 --> 00:04:33,830
Show me your score hand!

101
00:04:34,110 --> 00:04:35,560
Two fingers or more?

102
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:37,210
You beat the Buddies.

103
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:38,570
Three fingers?

104
00:04:38,850 --> 00:04:39,850
High five!

105
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:41,758
Did you spot the sparrow?

106
00:04:42,048 --> 00:04:44,708
It was on the railing, just outside the door!

107
00:04:45,035 --> 00:04:46,300
Here is a fun fact!

108
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,743
A sparrow's heart beats hundreds of times in one minute!

109
00:04:49,763 --> 00:04:52,940
Scientists count it again and again, to be sure.

110
00:04:53,244 --> 00:04:54,524
Tell someone at home!

111
00:04:56,413 --> 00:04:59,503
Next time: Pip pushes his fridge door gently.

112
00:04:59,773 --> 00:05:02,453
It swings shut by itself, and stays closed.

113
00:05:02,733 --> 00:05:04,623
There is no lock and no catch.

114
00:05:04,903 --> 00:05:06,293
What keeps it closed?

115
00:05:06,583 --> 00:05:08,403
One, the cold air inside.

116
00:05:08,683 --> 00:05:10,403
Two, a strip of magnet.

117
00:05:10,683 --> 00:05:12,053
Three, sticky glue.

118
00:05:12,333 --> 00:05:13,663
Lock in your answer!

119
00:05:14,534 --> 00:05:16,734
Let's read today's key words together!

120
00:05:17,024 --> 00:05:17,784
Ready?

121
00:05:18,071 --> 00:05:19,021
Prediction.

122
00:05:20,592 --> 00:05:22,142
Variable to be measured.

123
00:05:24,203 --> 00:05:25,123
Reliable.

124
00:05:26,609 --> 00:05:27,549
Well done!

125
00:05:28,531 --> 00:05:29,632
Say it with us!

126
00:05:30,432 --> 00:05:31,182
Ready?

127
00:05:31,306 --> 00:05:31,966
One!

128
00:05:32,306 --> 00:05:33,006
two!

129
00:05:33,311 --> 00:05:34,486
Now you know!
